+</span>
+<header>
+<h1>
+RUSTSEC-2026-0602
+</h1>
+<span class="subtitle">
+<p>Ed25519 signing key pairs with an inconsistent embedded public key are accepted</p>
+</span>
+</header>
+<dl>
+<dt id="reported">Reported</dt>
+<dd>
+<time datetime="2026-11-16">
+November 16, 2026
+</time>
+</dd>
+<dt id="issued">Issued</dt>
+<dd>
+<time datetime="2026-11-27">
+November 27, 2026
+</time>
+</dd>
+<dt id="package">Package</dt>
+<dd>
+<a href="/packages/dryoc.html">dryoc</a>
+(<a href="https://crates.io/crates/dryoc">crates.io</a>)
+</dd>
+<dt id="type">Type</dt>
+<dd>
+Vulnerability
+</dd>
+<dt id="categories">Categories</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<a href="/categories/crypto-failure.html">crypto-failure</a>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+<dt id="keywords">Keywords</dt>
+<dd>
+<a href="/keywords/ed25519.html">#ed25519</a>
+<a href="/keywords/signature.html">#signature</a>
+<a href="/keywords/key-recovery.html">#key-recovery</a>
+<a href="/keywords/keypair-validation.html">#keypair-validation</a>
+<a href="/keywords/serde.html">#serde</a>
+</dd>
+<dt id="aliases">Aliases</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vc3q-r4qh-vp64">GHSA-vc3q-r4qh-vp64</a>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+<dt id="details">References</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<a href="https://github.com/brndnmtthws/dryoc/pull/230">
+https://github.com/brndnmtthws/dryoc/pull/230
+</a>
+</li>
+<li>
+<a href="https://github.com/brndnmtthws/dryoc/releases/tag/v2.0.1">
+https://github.com/brndnmtthws/dryoc/releases/tag/v2.0.1
+</a>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+<dt id="patched">Patched</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<code>&#62;=2.0.1</code>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+</dl>
+<h3 id="description">Description</h3>
+<p>An Ed25519 secret key in <code>dryoc</code> is 64 bytes: the 32-byte seed followed by
+the 32-byte public key. When signing, the public key used in the challenge
+hash <code>k = H(R || A || M)</code> is read from bytes 32..64 of the secret key, while
+the nonce <code>r</code>, and therefore <code>R</code>, is derived only from the seed and the
+message.</p>
+<p>In affected versions, <code>SigningKeyPair::from_slices</code> and, when the <code>serde</code>
+feature is enabled, the derived <code>Deserialize</code> implementation of
+<code>SigningKeyPair</code> store the 64 secret-key bytes as given, without checking
+that the embedded public key matches the one derived from the seed. The
+separate <code>public_key</code> field of the struct is not used for signing, so only
+the embedded half is relevant. <code>SigningKeyPair::from_secret_key</code> re-derives
+the key pair from the seed and is not affected.</p>
+<p>If an attacker can replace bytes 32..64 of a stored secret key without being
+able to read the seed, and can obtain signatures over the same message from
+both the original and the modified key, the two signatures share the same <code>R</code>
+and differ in <code>S</code>. The private scalar can then be computed as
+<code>a = (S1 - S2) / (k1 - k2)</code>, which allows signatures to be forged for
+arbitrary messages under the original public key. Examples of such conditions
+are key storage without integrity protection, or an application that
+assembles the 64 secret-key bytes from two separate sources. The upstream
+advisory rates the issue as low severity.</p>
+<p>This behavior matches libsodium, which also trusts the embedded public key,
+so it is not a compatibility defect; the concern is that the higher-level
+constructors in <code>dryoc</code> did not enforce the consistency that
+<code>from_secret_key</code> already provides.</p>
+<h2>Fix</h2>
+<p>Version 2.0.1 validates imported Ed25519 key pairs in
+<code>SigningKeyPair::from_slices</code> and during serde deserialization, rejecting key
+material whose embedded or separate public key does not match the seed. In
+addition, <code>SigningKeyPair</code> signing and <code>Ed25519phSigner</code> finalization
+re-derive the embedded public key from the seed, since the key pair's fields
+remain publicly mutable.</p>
+<p>The Classic API (<code>dryoc::classic::crypto_sign</code>) keeps libsodium-compatible
+semantics: callers of functions such as <code>crypto_sign_detached</code> must supply
+consistent signing key material.</p>
+<p>Users should upgrade to <code>dryoc</code> 2.0.1 or later.</p>
